{
  "id": "COMPOUND:17694 - PROTEIN:6340",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Vitexin 2''-O-glucoside may interact with the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex through a series of molecular pathways involving CD70 antigen. The compound is linked to CD70, which is a target of multiple long-chain fatty acid derivatives, including (2S)-1-O-3,6,9,12,15-Octadecapentaenoyl-2-O-6,9,12,15-Octadecatetraenoyl-3-O-Beta-D-Galactopyranosylsn-Glycerol and (1S,4S,8R,9R,12S,13S,14S,16R,17R,19R)-9,19-dihydroxy-7,7-dimethyl-18-methylidene-3,10,15-trioxahexacyclo[15.2.1.01,13.04,12.08,12.014,16]icosan-2-one. These fatty acid derivatives are known to modulate the activity of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, which is a key component of the polycomb repressive complex 2 (PRC2) and is involved in epigenetic regulation. The evidence suggests that Vitexin 2''-O-glucoside may act as a modulator of this complex, potentially through the activation or inhibition of its enzymatic activity. The scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, but further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction between the compound and the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex.",
